This file is the source of truth for the project. Builder sessions read it before changing anything and add nothing it does not name. `CLAUDE.md` holds the rules for sessions and any amendments the owner has made to this spec.

Each section below starts with a note on what belongs there. Replace the note with the real content.

## Purpose and scope

What the project is for, who uses it, and what it does. Name what is out of scope as plainly as what is in.

## What done looks like

Numbered exit criteria that code can check. Each one names the command or test that decides it, so no criterion rests on a person's or a model's opinion.

## Architecture

The parts of the system, what each one is responsible for, and how they pass data to each other. Say which parts are plain code and which call a model.

## Contracts and data formats

The data that crosses each boundary: schemas, file formats and record layouts. Code validates everything against these.

## Fixed constraints and approval gates

Limits that hold for the whole build, such as budget, tools and access. Then the changes that need the owner's approval. The gated paths are listed in `CLAUDE.md`, "Approval gates".

## Open questions

Decisions not yet made. For each, say who decides and which build step it blocks. A builder session that reaches one stops and asks the owner.

## Build order and handoff

One row per step, in the order they are built. A step is small enough for one builder session, and its "done when" is something code can check. Every step ends with the full test suite passing.

